WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.259
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:11.160
How do people create these beautiful computer
generated images and videos that we see in

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.380
Hollywood blockbuster movies?

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.789
In the world of light simulation programs,
to obtain a photorealistic image, we create

00:00:18.789 --> 00:00:24.739
a digital copy of a scene, add a camera and
a light source, and simulate the paths of

00:00:24.739 --> 00:00:28.689
millions of light rays between the camera
and the light sources.

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:33.530
This technique we like to call path tracing
and it may take several minutes to obtain

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.640
only one image on a powerful computer.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:42.469
However, in these simulations, the rays of
light are allowed to bounce off of the surface

00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:43.739
of objects.

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:49.670
In reality, many objects are volumes, where
the rays of light can penetrate their surface

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.790
and scatter around before exiting or being
absorbed.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:59.690
Examples include not only rendering amazingly
huge smoke plumes and haze, but all kinds

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:05.990
of translucent objects, like our skin, marble,
wax, and many others.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:12.330
Such an extended simulation program we call
not path tracing but volumetric path tracing,

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:18.890
and we can create even more beautiful images
with it, however, this comes at a steep price:

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:24.280
if the classical path tracing took several
minutes per image, this addition of complexity

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.329
often bumps up the execution time to several
hours.

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:33.390
In order to save time, we have to realize
that not all light paths contribute equally

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.900
to our image.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:40.960
Many of them carry barely any contributions,
and only a tiny tiny fraction of these paths

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.119
carry the majority of the information that
we see in these images.

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:50.090
So what if we could create algorithms that
know exactly where to look for these high

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.280
value light paths and systematically focus
on them?

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.509
This family of techniques we call importance
sampling methods.

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:02.729
These help us finding the regions where light
is concentrated, if you will.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.720
This piece of work is an excellent new way
of doing importance sampling for volumetric

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:13.190
path tracing, and it works by identifying
and focusing on regions that are the most

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.020
likely to scatter light.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:19.750
And, it beats the already existing importance
sampling techniques with ease.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:25.660
Now, to demonstrate how simple this technique
is, a few years ago, during a discussion with

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:30.410
one of the authors, Marcos Fajado, I told
him that I would implement their method in

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:36.030
real time on the graphical card in a smaller
than four kilobyte program.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.070
So we made a bet.

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:44.370
Four kilobytes is so little, we can store
only a fraction of a second of mp3 music in

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:45.370
it.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:48.930
Also, this is an empty file generated with
Windows Word.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:54.010
Apparently, in some software systems, the
definition of "nothing" takes several times

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.750
more than 4 kilobytes.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:01.700
And after a bit of experimentation, I was
quite stunned by the results, because the

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:08.390
final result was less than 2 kilobytes, even
if support for some rudimentary animations

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:09.430
is added.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:15.270
The whole computer program that executes volumetric
path tracing with this equiangular importance

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:19.210
sampling technique fits on your business card...
twice.

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:21.070
Absolute insanity.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:24.320
I've put a link discussing some details in
the video description.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:29.490
Now, don't be fooled by the simplicity of
the presentation here, the heart and soul

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:35.790
of the algorithm that created the rocket launch
scene in Men in Black 3 is the same as this

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:36.790
one.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:41.070
Due to legal reasons it is not advisable to
show it to you in this video, but this is

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:45.480
fortunately one more excellent reason for
you to have a look at the paper!

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.170
As always, the link is available in the video
description.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:54.291
This is my favorite kind of paper, where there
are remarkably large gains to be had, and

00:03:54.291 --> 00:03:58.690
it can be easily added to pretty much any
light simulation program out there.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:04.600
I often like to say that the value/complexity
ratio is tending towards infinity.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.350
This work is a prime example of that.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:13.080
By the way, Marcos and his team recently won
a technical Oscar award, not only for this,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.620
but for their decades of hard work on their
Arnold renderer, which is behind many many

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:18.930
Hollywood productions.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.639
I've put a link to their website in the video
description as well, have a look!

00:04:22.639 --> 00:04:23.749
Congrats guys!

00:04:23.749 --> 00:04:27.949
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

